{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is an ad targeting system?

An ad targeting system decides which eligible advertisement to show for one opportunity,
such as an ad slot in an app or on a web page. It first removes campaigns that are not
allowed to compete, then predicts useful outcomes, chooses a winner through an auction,
and records what happened for measurement and future training.

This is an ML systems problem because a high score is never enough. The serving path must
meet its deadline while enforcing consent, campaign budget, frequency limits, and
fairness rules. The core invariant is simple: **do not serve an ad unless its hard
constraints can be verified.**
{% /section-card %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Eligibility is a gate" eyebrow="Correctness" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Consent, inventory rules, budget, frequency caps, and policy exclusions run before a
model score can influence the auction.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Ranking is a prediction" eyebrow="ML task" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Estimate a calibrated probability of a useful outcome from approved context and
features, then combine it with a bid under explicit pacing rules.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Logging closes the loop" eyebrow="Operations" icon="database" tone="cyan" %}
Log the candidate set, positions, decisions, and outcomes asynchronously so measurement
does not hold the user-facing response open.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the ML problem and business context" %}


### Start with one decision, not a vague personalization goal

For every eligible impression, choose at most one campaign and return a bid before the
exchange deadline. The advertiser wants efficient spend and reach; the publisher wants
relevant, policy-compliant ads; and the person viewing the page expects their choices
and limits to be respected.

**In scope**

- Consent-aware contextual and first-party audience targeting.
- Campaign eligibility, frequency caps, daily pacing, ranking, and a second-price-like
  auction with a transparent reserve policy.
- Impression, click, and conversion logging for reporting and controlled model updates.

**Out of scope for the first version**

- Cross-site identity graphs built from unconsented data.
- Automatic creative generation or unconstrained reinforcement learning on live spend.
- A claim that clicks alone prove incremental business value.

### Name the costly mistakes

- **Serving without verified consent** is a privacy and policy failure, not a relevance
  trade-off. Fail closed for the affected targeting signal.
- **Overspending a campaign** breaks the advertiser contract. A late budget update must
  never become permission to keep bidding.
- **Optimizing click-through rate alone** can overexpose a group, reward misleading
  creative, or concentrate impressions in a narrow audience.
- **Timing out an auction** loses the opportunity. A bounded contextual fallback is
  acceptable only after the same hard gates pass.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Before discussing models, write down the auction deadline, the regions where data may
be processed, the daily budget rule, the frequency-cap window, and the protected or
high-risk slices that require separate evaluation. These are product requirements that
the architecture must preserve.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="estimates" title="2. Back-of-the-envelope data and compute estimates" %}


### Make the serving estimate concrete

Assume one region handles **250,000 eligible auction requests per second** at peak. Each
request starts with 200 candidates after coarse matching, then sends only the best 40
to a richer scorer. A 100 ms end-to-end exchange deadline leaves a 45 ms internal
budget after network and serialization allowance.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="250K" label="Peak auctions / second" detail="Regional qualified auction load" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="50M" label="Candidates / second" detail="250K auctions times 200 coarse candidates" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="10M" label="Rich scores / second" detail="250K auctions times 40 rerank candidates" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="45 ms" label="Internal deadline" detail="Eligibility, feature joins, score, auction, and response" tone="rose" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Targeting and auction capacity lab

Precomputed, consent-safe features let the auction avoid expensive online joins. Move
the cache control and watch a fixed peak auction load move between the fast path and
the online feature-plus-rich-scoring path. The two branch values always sum to the
source load; the warning marks the point where the online fleet loses planned headroom.

{% traffic-split dataFile="/api/content/practice/ad-targeting/data/auction-feature-capacity.json" /%}

At 92% cache coverage, about **20,000 auctions/second** use online features. At 80%,
that becomes **50,000 auctions/second**. Plan the feature and scoring fleet for the
lower coverage state, not the average dashboard value. A cached feature is usable only
when its consent state, feature version, and freshness window still match the request.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="framing" title="3. Formal ML framing" %}


### Define the prediction and its constraints

For an eligible candidate campaign `c` and approved request context `x`, predict a
calibrated probability of the chosen outcome inside a declared horizon: `P(conversion
within 7 days | x, c, shown)`. A practical ranking score is expected value, for example
`bid(c) * P(conversion)`, then adjusted by an explicit pacing multiplier. Policy,
budget, and frequency constraints are gates, not learned penalties that the model may
choose to ignore.

**Use features that could exist at decision time**

- Page or app context, placement, locale, device class, and time bucket.
- Campaign configuration, approved creative metadata, remaining budget, and exposure
  count from an authoritative capped counter.
- Consented first-party interest features with an as-of timestamp and retention policy.

**Avoid two common training errors**

- Training on clicks without the impression position and candidate set confuses model
  quality with the old ranker's exposure bias.
- Joining conversions or profile values that arrived after the impression creates
  leakage. Every training row needs an event-time feature snapshot and a label cutoff.

### Baseline before complexity

Begin with contextual eligibility plus a calibrated gradient-boosted CTR or conversion
model, budget pacing, and a deterministic auction. Compare it with contextual-only
ranking on consent, geography, device, new-user, campaign-size, and frequency slices.
Only add embeddings or exploration after the baseline is observable and reversible.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="4. End-to-end architecture: data, training, and serving" %}


### Separate the response path from the learning path

{% system-flow title="Consent-aware auction path" caption="The synchronous path makes one bounded decision. Events leave asynchronously for attribution, evaluation, and training." %}
{% system-node title="Request gateway" eyebrow="Validate" icon="check" tone="blue" %}
Normalize the bid request, attach a deadline and region, verify consent and inventory,
then discard fields the auction is not permitted to use.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Eligibility service" eyebrow="Filter" icon="filter" tone="rose" %}
Match campaigns, enforce targeting rules, frequency caps, budget reservations, and
creative policy before ranking. It returns an auditable candidate set.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Feature and rank" eyebrow="Predict" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Read versioned online features, score a bounded set, apply pacing multipliers, and
return scores with model and feature versions.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Auction and response" eyebrow="Choose" icon="scale" tone="amber" %}
Apply reserve price and final policy checks, atomically reserve spend, return the winner,
and include a trace ID for later explanation.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

The event stream is outside the deadline. It stores the full eligible candidate set,
ranked positions, winning price, policy version, consent state, and outcome events.
Stream jobs build point-in-time features and attribution tables. Offline training reads
only versioned snapshots; a model registry records training data range, slices, and
approval evidence before deployment.

{% code-block file="/api/content/practice/ad-targeting/code/rtb-bid-request.json" language="json" title="Bounded bid request" /%}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deep-dive" title="5. Deep dive on model, policy, and auction decisions" %}


### Policy, fairness, and failure decision lab

Choose a scenario, then select a node to inspect its responsibility. This lab shows why
policy and fairness evaluation remain on the request path while observability and model
training stay asynchronous. A system may degrade from personalized to contextual
ranking; it may not invent consent, ignore a frequency cap, or serve when a required
eligibility check is unavailable.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/practice/ad-targeting/data/policy-fairness-failure-lab.json" /%}

### Make the auction deterministic at the boundary

The ranker produces a score, not permission to spend. After final eligibility, choose
the winning campaign using a documented pricing rule, atomically reserve a small budget
amount, and emit a decision record. If the reservation cannot complete before the
deadline, remove that campaign and continue with other eligible candidates. Never
charge based on a score that was computed before a campaign became exhausted.

{% code-block file="/api/content/practice/ad-targeting/code/rtb-bid-response.json" language="json" title="Bid decision response" /%}

**Cold start and skew controls**

- A new campaign can use contextual features and conservative bid caps until enough
  representative exposure exists; do not manufacture a behavioral profile.
- Downweight or cap repeated exposure per person and report reach, frequency, and
  outcome quality by campaign and audience slice.
- Keep a small randomized exploration allocation only where consent and product policy
  allow it, with a fixed spend ceiling and holdout reporting.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="evaluation" title="6. Evaluation and QA strategy" %}


### Evaluate the funnel, not just a click metric

Offline, check calibration, AUC or log loss, and ranking quality on impression groups
with position-aware or randomized data. Measure prediction quality separately for
contextual-only traffic, new users, regions, device classes, campaign sizes, and
protected or high-risk product slices where allowed by policy. A model cannot be
approved from an aggregate lift that hides a harmful slice regression.

**Online release gates**

- Primary: incremental conversions or advertiser value measured with a holdout, not
  attributed conversions alone.
- User and publisher: frequency distribution, complaint rate, creative-policy escapes,
  and page latency.
- System: p95/p99 auction latency, timeout rate, cache coverage, feature freshness,
  budget-reservation conflicts, and serving cost.
- Fairness and privacy: delivery and outcome disparities on approved slices, consent
  mismatch rate, and use of restricted targeting fields.

### Test data integrity before a model test

Replay captured consent-valid requests against the candidate service. Contract-test
feature defaults and schema versions, compare online values with the training snapshot,
and test that an expired consent string, exhausted budget, and failed cap counter each
remove a candidate. Audit impression logs for duplicates, missing position, delayed
conversions, and missing policy versions before trusting an A/B result.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="7. Operations and retraining plan" %}


### Operate with owners and stop conditions

The auction owner watches deadline, error, and budget-reservation health. The ML owner
watches feature freshness, calibration, slice quality, and training-data drift. The
policy owner watches consent validation, creative-rule changes, and appeals or
complaints. Each alert needs a preapproved action, not only a dashboard.

**Page immediately**

- Required consent, frequency-cap, or budget-reservation checks are unavailable or
  returning inconsistent results.
- Any region exceeds its hard auction deadline or a campaign overspends its tolerance.
- A policy mismatch or restricted-feature use is detected.

**Retrain and release deliberately**

- Build daily point-in-time training snapshots; retrain only when label maturity,
  coverage, and data-quality gates pass.
- Shadow-score first, then canary a small traffic share with the same hard filters and
  an instant rollback to the prior model.
- Freeze promotion when calibration, a protected slice, privacy metrics, or serving
  latency breaches a release gate even if aggregate revenue rises.

{% code-block file="/api/content/practice/ad-targeting/code/consent-api.http" language="http" title="Consent is an explicit service boundary" /%}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="8. Trade-offs and future improvements" %}


### Choose the trade-off consciously

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Contextual first" eyebrow="Privacy" icon="search" tone="green" %}
Uses page and placement context with less personal data. It is a robust fallback, but
can be less precise for returning users.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Richer online features" eyebrow="Quality" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Can improve calibration and conversion prediction, but adds freshness, latency, and
consent-propagation risk to the serving path.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Strict fail-closed gates" eyebrow="Safety" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Protects privacy and spend when an authority fails, but may reduce fill rate and short
term revenue during an incident.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Extend only after the invariant is stable

Possible next steps include privacy-preserving aggregate measurement, causal lift models,
creative diversity constraints, on-device interest signals, and contextual exploration.
Each needs a separate data-use review, counterfactual evaluation plan, and rollback
condition. Do not add a more complex model to compensate for missing consent, unreliable
budget state, or biased labels.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
The useful final interview answer is not "maximize CTR." It is: serve the best eligible
ad within a bounded deadline, preserve budget and consent invariants, and prove that
quality gains survive counterfactual, operational, and fairness checks.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

